hat dominates the blend, or increase the new model's blend weight first to make hyperparameter differences detectable."}, "steps": ["29588171:13->14", "29588171:14->15", "29588171:15->16", "29588171:17->18", "29588171:18->19", "29588171:19->20", "29588171:21->22", "29588171:22->23", "29588171:28->29", "29588171:31->32", "29588171:32->33"]}, {"instance": {"goal": "Why do many of the notebook runs fail or produce unexpected outputs during this hyperparameter search?", "context": "Multiple transitions were spent fixing bugs: toggling predict vs predict_proba, commenting/uncommenting model.fit, fixing missing target variable in fit call, uncommenting the lightgbm import. These housekeeping fixes consumed roughly half the 21 transitions.", "action": "Each fix was a single-line toggle (comment/uncomment) discovered only after a failed run. The author had to iterate through v16→v17 (predict_proba fix), v20→v21 (predict fix), v25→v26 (missing y), v26→v27 (missing import), v27→v28 and v29→v30 (fit call toggles)."}, "general": {"goal": "How can wasted iterations from trivial code errors be minimized when rapidly exploring model configurations?", "context": "When experimenting by commenting/uncommenting code blocks to switch between configurations, it is easy to leave imports disabled, pass wrong arguments, or use the wrong prediction method, each costing a full run cycle.", "action": "Keep all configuration variants as named options controlled by a single flag variable (e.g., a config dict) rather than commenting/uncommenting lines. Validate the pipeline locally with a tiny data subset before launching a full run, so import errors, missing arguments, and shape mismatches surface in seconds rather than after a full training cycle."}, "steps": ["29588171:16->17", "29588171:20->21", "29588171:25->26", "29588171:26->27", "29588171:27->28", "29588171:29->30"]}, {"instance": {"goal": "Does training the LightGBM model on the full labeled dataset (X, y) instead of a train split (X_train, y_train) improve the leaderboard score?", "context": "The author toggled between model.fit(X_train, y_train) and model.fit(X, y) multiple times. The final submitted version (v34) used full data and scored 0.79980, slightly higher than v32/v33 at 0.79972.", "action": "Switched to fitting on the full labeled set (no holdout) for the final submission model, gaining +0.00008 on LB. This is consistent with the model being blended at low weight, so overfitting risk is mitigated by the dominant external predictions."}, "general": {"goal": "When a model is used only as a minor blend component, does reserving a validation split still help or does training on all labeled data produce a better submission?", "context": "The model contributes a small fraction of the final blended prediction, so its individual overfitting has limited impact on the ensemble. A validation split reduces training data without providing actionable early-stopping benefit at such low blend weight.", "action": "When a model's blend weight is very small and early stopping is not used, train on the full labeled set to maximize the quality of its small contribution. Reserve a validation split only when the model's weight is large enough that overfitting would materially hurt the blend."}, "steps": ["29588171:24->25", "29588171:33->34"]}], "error": null, "raw": null, "model": "wine-claude-opus-4-6"}
